# docs/adr/0042. Automatic HTTPS via Let's Encrypt — Caddy issues and
# renews certificates for each of these on its own, no separate certbot
# step. Requires DOMAIN's DNS (and the admin./api. subdomains) to already
# point at this server before it starts, or issuance fails.
#
# Not the only way to front this stack: anyone with their own reverse
# proxy (Traefik, nginx, an existing shared one) can drop this whole
# `caddy` service from docker-compose.prod.yml and point their own proxy
# at the api/public-site/editor-app containers' internal ports instead
# (3000/4321/80).
#
# Two directives, each in an environment variable of its own and empty when the
# variable is not set, so that one file serves two deployments:
#   KOMETIO_ACME_EMAIL_LINE  "email you@example.com": Let's Encrypt's contact
#       address. The compose stack sets it (docker-compose.prod.yml requires
#       ACME_EMAIL); the single image does when ACME_EMAIL is given, and goes
#       without it otherwise, which Let's Encrypt allows.
#   KOMETIO_ACCESS_LOG  "true" or "false" (the image maps anything else to
#       "false", and the API refuses it): a line for each request (see
#       access_log_true below), or none. It is the variable the API reads for
#       its own lines (docs/adr/0122), so that one setting is the whole log of a
#       request, from the API and from the proxy in front of it. It is read as
#       the name of a snippet, `access_log_<value>`: a value that is neither
#       stops Caddy with "File to import not found", and an empty one does too,
#       which is why the compose file says `${KOMETIO_ACCESS_LOG:-false}`.
#   KOMETIO_CADDY_OPTIONS  "admin off" and "skip_install_trust", on two lines:
#       the single image sets them. Caddy's admin API listens on localhost and,
#       in the compose stack, nothing else shares that container; in the image
#       the public site runs the code of a theme in the same one, and must not be
#       able to reconfigure the proxy. And a server has no business putting a
#       certificate authority into its own trust store, which Caddy tries to do
#       for a local one.
# What a request's line is allowed to carry (docs/adr/0126). It is what is left
# after the rest is deleted, not after the secrets are:
#
#   - no request header, so no cookie, no Authorization, no token a client
#     chose a header for, and no browser or Referer;
#   - no response header, so no Set-Cookie;
#   - no address of the client or the port it came from;
#   - the URI without its query, where a preview link's token and a reset
#     link's live.
#
# That leaves the time, method, host, path, status, size and duration, which is
# what is asked of a log of this kind (what was slow, what was refused, what
# was not found). A person who needs the address of a client (an abuse report)
# has a proxy of their own to put it in: this is the one we ship.
#
# It is above the global options because a snippet is not found from inside a
# block that comes before it.
(request_log_format) {
	format filter {
		wrap json
		request>headers delete
		resp_headers delete
		request>remote_ip delete
		request>remote_port delete
		request>client_ip delete
		request>tls delete
		request>uri regexp \?.* ""
	}
}

{
	{$KOMETIO_CADDY_OPTIONS:}
	{$KOMETIO_ACME_EMAIL_LINE:}

	# Caddy's own log, which is also where the line of a request goes when it
	# is for a name that has no site here (the machine's address, a scanner's
	# guess, the redirect from port 80), and where an error's line carries the
	# request that caused it. Left as it comes, that is every header and the
	# address, in a line the sites' own filter never sees; with a `log` in any
	# site block it is on even when the access log is not asked for. The same
	# format, so that nothing a request carried is in any line.
	log {
		import request_log_format
	}
}

# Compression is the proxy's job, not the apps': neither the Astro node
# server nor nginx compresses on its own here, so without this every page,
# stylesheet and script went out uncompressed — the HTML of a content-heavy
# page is several times its gzipped size. zstd first, gzip for whatever does
# not speak it. Caddy skips what is already compressed (images, video, fonts)
# and anything too small to be worth it.
(compressed) {
	encode zstd gzip
}

# Set only where the app has not set its own (`?`): the public site sends
# `no-referrer` from a preview, whose address carries a token, and that
# choice must stand. No includeSubDomains in the HSTS header: this stack
# owns four names under DOMAIN, not every name there is.
#
# One `header` per field, not one block: in a block, Caddy joins the `?`
# conditions into one ("all three absent"), so a page that set its own
# Referrer-Policy got no HSTS and no nosniff either.
(security_headers) {
	header ?Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	header ?X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
	header ?Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
}

# One line for each request, to the proxy's output (docs/adr/0126). The switch
# is the name of the snippet that is imported: with `false` there is no `log`
# directive in any site, and Caddy writes no line of a request, as it never did.
(access_log_true) {
	log {
		output stdout
		import request_log_format
	}
}
(access_log_false) {
}

{$DOMAIN}, www.{$DOMAIN} {
	import security_headers
	import access_log_{$KOMETIO_ACCESS_LOG:false}
	import compressed
	reverse_proxy {$SITE_UPSTREAM:public-site:4321}
}

admin.{$DOMAIN} {
	import security_headers
	import access_log_{$KOMETIO_ACCESS_LOG:false}
	import compressed
	reverse_proxy {$EDITOR_UPSTREAM:editor-app:80}
}

api.{$DOMAIN} {
	import security_headers
	import access_log_{$KOMETIO_ACCESS_LOG:false}
	# The page generator's server-sent events pass through uncompressed and
	# as they are written: `encode` leaves text/event-stream alone (checked
	# on 2026-09-29 against caddy:2-alpine, audit B15).
	import compressed
	reverse_proxy {$API_UPSTREAM:api:3000}
}
